  <h2>Project Overview</h2>
  <p>
    As a part of my high school engineering course, we were each challenged for our capstone project to work with the organization May We Help to make a Halloween costume for a child with a disability. 
Often times, children with disabilities are not able to experience the same events as other children, which can lead them to not feel normal. One of those events is Halloween. Often their disability 
limits the costume they are able to dress up as for Halloween.
  </p>

  <h2>My Role</h2>
  <p>
    The child that my team and I was workign with was wheelchair bound and wanted to be a backhoe, or as he called it: a "digger," for Halloween. We spent much time making the costume so that it fit to 
his wheelchair and so that it was just as intrciate as any other costume. However, we spent the majority fo our time making the actual arm of the backhoe move using a linear actuator at the touch of a button. 
This made the costume stand out and allowed us to put our engineering skills to use. This project was amazing and seeing the smile on the child's face when we were able to attach it to his wheelchair on 
Halloween meant everything. 
  </p>

  <h2>Results and Lessons Learned</h2>
  <p>
    This project was one of my first engineering projects and it helped me to develop some electrical engineering experience, soldering experience, and experience in the form of collaboration, problem-solving, 
and empathy in engineering.
  </p>

